=== run 1 ===
bench_mbtcp_pilot  K=10 rounds x 50 writes
  plc=127.0.0.1:56387  null-relays=127.0.0.1:56389 -> 127.0.0.1:56388  gateway=127.0.0.1:56390  agent=127.0.0.1:56391
(each round reports its own median; the band below is over the 10 round medians)

  arm                                   median     spread
  1 hop   HMI -> PLC                      45 us    +/-24%
  3 hops  HMI -> 2 null relays -> PLC     176 us    +/-42%
  3 hops  HMI -> agent -> gw -> PLC      468 us    +/-21%
          sign+verify+gate, no sockets     258 us    +/-25%

  vs a direct write:           423 us  (10.4x)
  of which the two hops:       131 us  (what any two middleboxes cost here)
  of which OASIS:              292 us  (2.7x the same shape without it)

  cross-check: OASIS's share 292 us vs sign+verify+gate alone 258 us -> +34 us unaccounted
  the accounting closes: OASIS's added latency is its crypto and its gate, not plumbing.

  Connections are reused on all three links (agent->gateway, gateway->PLC) and the
  clock is refreshed once per 1000 ms via part K's TimeView rather than per write.
  Before that, this bench measured 3172 us +/-724%: a connect per write is not just
  slower, it makes the latency unpredictable and burns ephemeral ports.
